    "text": "Production\n\n2026\n\nCalf crop production in 2026 is forecast to remain flat at 8.4 million head. Continued investment in improved genetics and technology is offset by the structural damage left by recent disease, drought, and the continued suspension of live cattle exports to the U.S. market due to New World Screwworm (NWS). Despite ranchers retaining heifers to rebuild herds, it takes roughly two to three years for a newly produced calf to enter the market. The 2026 calf crop is restricted by the small number of fertile cows currently on the ground.\n\nFaced with a closed border and the loss of premium export markets, producers are pivoting toward a long-term domestic strategy. While the uptick in 2026 slaughter numbers forecast a temporary liquidation or a clearing of older stock, producers are expected to ultimately focus efforts on the rebuilding of herds. Ranchers are expected to rely more heavily on the domestic market, as current price floors remain high enough to justify the overhead associated with herd expansion. By leveraging genetic improvements, producers aim to ensure that when the herd eventually recovers, the output is higher-quality, lower-cost protein that can compete effectively domestically.\n\n2025\n\nCalf crop production in 2025 is estimated to decrease 2 percent to 8.4 million head. The decline in the Mexican calf crop is primarily the result of herd liquidation following years of environmental and economic stress. By 2025, the cumulative effects of drought forced producers to sell off breeding stock, leading to fewer births in the current cycle.\n\nThe multi-year drought (2023–2024) that devastated northern and central Mexico left reservoir levels in key states such as Sinaloa and Sonora at 30-year lows, falling to just 13 percent of capacity. Insufficient grasslands and water to support large herds led to ranchers selling a record number of heifers to the United States in 2024 rather than keeping them for breeding.\n\nWith production costs exceeding the projected value of a future calf, many producers opted to market heifers into the U.S. market rather than retain them. Export sales provided critical liquidity needed to sustain the core breeding herd through the drought. As a result, Mexico exported approximately 1.25 million head to the United States in 2024, the highest level on record. This substantially reduced future reproductive capacity.\n\nSlaughter\n\n2026\n\nSlaughter is forecast to increase 5 percent in 2026 to 7.23 million head. After the 3 percent contraction in 2025, cattle slaughter in Mexico is projected to rebound sharply in 2026. This increase signals a correction caused by the backlog of animals retained during the 2025 border closure. In 2025, many ranchers kept cattle on feed longer with expectations of the reopening of the U.S. border. Cattle that would have been processed in late 2025 are being pushed into the first half of 2026 at higher weights.\n\n2025\n\nSlaughter is estimated to decline 3 percent in 2025 to 6.86 million heads. Although early forecasts suggested that the late-2024 U.S. border closure would prompt ranchers to divert cattle into domestic slaughterhouses, revised estimates indicate otherwise. Instead, larger and better-capitalized operations appear to have retained cattle on feed, opting to wait rather than market animals into depressed or volatile domestic prices. Retention of cattle on-site increased weights and delayed slaughter, pushing a share of expected 2025 slaughter into 2026. Additionally, as the calf crop declined, the price of replacement animals rose. Ranchers who might have slaughtered older cows decided to keep them for one more breeding cycle, given affordability limitations for new heifers.\n\nTrade\n\nImports\n\n2026\n\nCattle imports are forecast to decrease 67 percent in 2026 to 10,000 head. This sharp contraction is driven by heightened biosecurity concerns and a shifting regulatory landscape. The continued presence of NWS in Central America has forced more stringent sanitary protocols. These rigorous requirements have substantially increased the logistical and financial burden on importers, diminishing the economic incentive to source live cattle from the region.\n\nIn response to the 2025 import cycle from Nicaragua, which triggered significant domestic controversy and media scrutiny, Mexican authorities have tightened oversight. The 2025 imports were perceived by industry stakeholders and northern ranching cooperatives as an unacceptable risk factor for introducing NWS into Mexico’s critical northern export zones.\n\n2025\n\nCattle imports are estimated to remain flat in 2025 at 30,000 head due to the re-emergence of the NWS in Central America and its subsequent spread into Mexico. To prevent the northward spread of the parasite, Mexico implemented heightened sanitary restrictions on its southern border. Any risk of detection results in immediate shipment rejection. Ranchers and importers have little incentive to increase volume, as the risk of potential\n\n<ts></ts>",
