    "text": "Historically, oilseed production has offered better profitability compared to grains. Contacts indicated, if the growth in global fuel and fertilizer prices continues well into calendar year (CY) 2026, some farmers might switch from grains to oilseeds. Industry also noted medium and large farmers have already purchased the necessary inputs for spring planting and have prepared their fields for a specific crop. This will limit any significant deviations in perspective plantings for MY2026/27. However, contacts note small farmers are more likely to make their planting decisions later; therefore, Post will make any necessary oilseed adjustments based on CY2026 MEEA planting data.\n\nPost estimates MY2026/27 total oilseed area at 8.6 million hectares (ha), which is equivalent to the five-year average. According to SSSU official data, 1.3 million ha of winter rapeseed has already been planted. This area is 11 percent higher than in MY2025/26. Post accepts this number as the initial estimate for MY2026/27 rapeseed area, as spring rapeseed areas are minimal. The remainder of the total estimated oilseed area will be split between two spring crops: sunflower and soybeans. Imports of sunflower seeds are higher ahead of MY2026/27 planting season compared to the previous MY (Figure 1). Some of these volumes might have been imported for domestic processing to compensate for a shortage of domestic sunflower seeds in MY2025/26; however, there is an increase in imports from destinations that historically supply sunflower seeds for planting. Post uses this as a valid argument to increase its MY2026/27 sunflower area estimate to 5.4 million ha, a 6 percent increase compared to Post’s previous MY estimate.\n\nBased on the total estimated oilseed area, Post forecasts MY2026/27 soybean area at 1.8 million ha, an 11 percent decrease against Post’s previous MY estimate.\n\nAt the time of report writing, the normalized difference vegetation index (NDVI) was close to the long-term average (Figure 2). This indicates there are no significant issues for winter crops, specifically winter rapeseed. Note that NDVI is a standardized measure of vegetation. High values indicate healthier vegetation, while low values indicate low or no vegetation.\n\nMid-March 2026, the beginning of planting season, featured sufficient amounts of both surface and sub-surface moisture (Figures 3 and 4). This indicates plants will have good conditions immediately after planting and a sufficient amount of moisture after germination.\n\n<ts></ts>",
